  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLatest surface analysis places a cold front along the St\nLawrence Valley southwestward into the Great Lakes. Temperatures\nwill likely remain in the 60s through early this morning setting\na high floor for temperatures today before the cold front\ncrosses this afternoon and evening. Despite skies turning mostly\ncloudy by peak heating temperatures today are still on track to\nclimb into the upper 70s to low 80s south of the mountains. More\nin the way of clouds this morning and shower chances arriving\nthis morning will limit highs along and north of the mountains\nto the 60s. Latest CAM guidance focuses the best chances for\nshowers along and north of the mountains this morning with\nshowers struggling to hold together as the progress towards the\ncoast this afternoon. Based on latest trends many areas south of\nthe mountains may fail to realize measurable rainfall. For those\nthat do see some showers QPF amounts will generally be a tenth\nof inch or less with isolated higher amounts if any convective\nprocesses can materialize.\n\nThe front will be pushing offshore around sunset with skies\nclearing tonight. This will likely allow for valley fog tonight\nwith lows ranging from the upper 30s north to near 50 degrees\nalong the coast and southern NH. High pressure will build in\nSaturday for fair weather with highs ranging from the upper 50s\nnorth to upper 60s south. High pressure cresting over the area\nSaturday night will allow for favorable radiational cooling \nconditions that could allow for lows into the upper 20s north \nwith frost potential expanding south of the foothills.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn amplified trough approaches the region late Sunday, dragging a \ncold front through New England on Monday. Despite a rather strong \ntemperature gradient, it looks like a bit of a glancing blow with \nthe surface low and best dynamics well to the north up in Quebec. \nGenerally looks like two embedded precipitation chances:     \n    \n1.) Southern stream energy possibly clipping the southern half of \nthe forecast area Sunday night, although about half of current \nsolutions keep the rain fully south of our forecast area.\n\n2.) Along the cold front during the day Monday, best chance will be \nacross northern NH and NW Maine closest to the best forcing.\n\nNeither wave is a slam dunk or expected to bring significant soaking \nrains due to quick shortwave motions and a lack of deep moisture. In \nfact, there is the chance a sizable chunk of the forecast area sees \na dry frontal passage with the two waves splitting the area. \n\nThe real cool down arrives by Tuesday morning. Very likely the \ncoldest weather since the spring arrives Tuesday-Wednesday with 850 \nmb temperatures plunging to between 0 and -7C by Tuesday morning \ndepending on model of choice. Even 925 mb temperatures are near or \nslightly below freezing on guidance. NBM currently shows lows \nreaching the upper 20s-low 30s in the interior with 30s all the way \nto the coast, which would easily be the coldest morning of the fall \nso far. However these low temperatures are contingent on the \nboundary layer decoupling, and guidance is currently indicating 40-\n50+ kts at 850 mb driven by a strong pressure gradient, so would say \nthe temperature forecast here is not yet high confidence.\n\nWon't take much mixing on Tuesday to tap the stronger winds aloft, \nso can expect a blustery day, and with highs that will struggle to \nhit 60, there will be a fall chill in the air.\n\nDry and sunny conditions should last through Wednesday with high \npressure cresting overhead. Likely will see another cold start \ndespite 850 mb temps not as cold as better decoupling will be likely \nwith a slacking pressure gradient, but strong surface heating should \nonce again allow for comfortable daytime temperatures.",
